Exactly. The issue is fit, not realism.

Jazz is a good analogy because jazz can violate an enormous number of ordinary expectations and still feel right. The player establishes a key, pulse, motif, or harmonic language first. Then the strange note works because it has a relationship to something stable. Pure randomness is not jazz just because it contains unexpected notes.

That gives us a better model for images:

\text{magic}
=
\text{coherence}
+
\text{controlled deviation}

Not:

\text{magic}
=
\text{maximum weirdness}

The Wild Hawg image had too many instruments soloing simultaneously: colossal horn, battlefield, banners, mountains, glowing metal, ornate boar, gummies, product pouch, Celtic patterns, badges, giant typography, atmospheric effects. Individually, almost everything was attractive. Collectively, there wasn’t enough hierarchy.

Your Goldilocks point is the important one. We need modulation across several axes without maxing all of them at once: realism, ornament, scale, contrast, mythology, color, texture, motion, symbolism, and information density.

A good composition might behave more like:

70\% \text{stable}
+
20\% \text{stylized}
+
10\% \text{impossible}

Not because those exact percentages are sacred, but because the strange element becomes more powerful when most of the world gives it something to violate.

So imagine a Wild Hawg ad where the pouch is convincingly real. The rock, fog, forest, and light are nearly photographic. Then the one impossible move is that the brass boar horn is subtly emerging from the fog behind it, enormous but partially concealed. Now your eye says:

Wait—was that actually there?

That is much stronger than screaming “FANTASY!” from twenty different directions.

Same with color. We don’t need every attractive color. Establish a tonal key:

\text{charcoal}
+
\text{forest green}
+
\text{aged brass}

Then perhaps one small accent—amber gummy translucence.

Everything else stays within that harmonic neighborhood.

And the same rule applies to textures. If the bag is matte, the horn is polished brass, the gummies are translucent sugar, and the background is soft atmospheric fog, that’s already four distinct material voices. We probably don’t also need glowing runes, molten gold, carved stone lettering, sparks, leather banners, polished shields, and wet moss all demanding equal attention.

I’d turn this into a standing visual rule:

One anchor, one melody, one surprise.

For Wild Hawg:

Anchor: the product.
Melody: brass-war-horn / wild-boar visual language.
Surprise: one reality bend.

Then everything else supports those three.

And when we do mix abstraction levels, they need voice leading between them. A photographic pouch can transition into an illustrated background if the lighting, palette, edge treatment, grain, perspective, and shadows gradually bridge the two. You don’t want photographic → fantasy painting → UI graphic → 3D sculpture with hard seams between each ontology.

That’s the equivalent of changing musical keys without modulation.

So I think the deeper rule is:

\boxed{\text{Difference needs transition}}

The farther two elements are apart—in realism, scale, color, texture, era, symbolism—the stronger the bridge between them has to be.

That’s where the magic lives: not in avoiding dissonance, but in making the dissonance belong.